WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:08.400
For more than 800 years, Notre Dame Cathedral in&nbsp;
Paris has stood at the center of European history.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.640
But beyond its historical significance,&nbsp;
what makes this cathedral so remarkable?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.760
And what gives its architecture such an&nbsp;
extraordinary sense of harmony and unity?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.680
According to medieval chronicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.960
the façade of Notre Dame Cathedral&nbsp;
struck those who beheld it with terror:

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.240
Quae mole sua terrorem incutit spectantibus.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.280
It’s important to be aware of how a medieval&nbsp;
person would have experienced the cathedral.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.320
Paris looked very different back then—you would&nbsp;
not approach the cathedral through the broad open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.920
forecourt from which we see it today, with a&nbsp;
full, unobstructed view of the front façade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:56.000
from a distance; instead, you would approach Notre&nbsp;
Dame Cathedral through a parvis—a small, irregular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.040
open space immediately in front of the church;&nbsp;
most of the buildings were timber structures of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:11.760
three or four stories, packed together in a&nbsp;
densely populated city with narrow streets.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.760
Upper floors would project outwards—neighboring&nbsp;
houses could nearly touch above the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.960
sunlight was reduced, and airflow was poor.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.960
As you walked these dark alleys on&nbsp;
your way to the city’s cathedral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.000
making your way through the crowd, you&nbsp;
would suddenly emerge into the parvis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:41.360
directly before the cathedral, where you could&nbsp;
finally draw a breath and see the light of day.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.840
Back then you would only see the façade&nbsp;
of Notre Dame from a very close distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.400
standing in this small plaza&nbsp;
adjacent to the church front.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.280
You would see the cathedral raised&nbsp;
above the level of the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.720
on top of a flight of steps, and the&nbsp;
immense verticality of the building in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.080
contrast with the small wooden structures&nbsp;
of the city would instantly overwhelm you.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.280
Those chroniclers who said that the great mass&nbsp;
of the church would strike you with terror were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.240
really referring to the disproportion felt&nbsp;
between the human body and the monumental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:22.720
scale of the building, especially perceived&nbsp;
when you’re put right in front of the façade.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.120
To answer why the west façade&nbsp;
of Notre Dame is so harmonious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.240
we first need to identify its main elements:

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.720
The façade is governed by a balance&nbsp;
between vertical and horizontal lines.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.680
Each level is organized in&nbsp;
three individual sections.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.320
The two lower levels have openings for portals&nbsp;
and windows that emphasize the middle axes.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.400
The rose window in the center of&nbsp;
the second level forms a halo for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:59.120
the statues of Notre Dame (Our Lady), crowned&nbsp;
and holding the child, flanked by two angels.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.640
At the same time, this three part organization&nbsp;
is linked together by two horizontal elements:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:18.000
the royal gallery, a series of statues in&nbsp;
niches that depict the biblical kings of Judah;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:26.240
and the pierced screen that connects the&nbsp;
two towers, lightening up the upper walls.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.840
The whole composition is&nbsp;
symmetrical at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.720
but on closer examination, you&nbsp;
can notice small variations:

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:52.480
The three portals are all different sizes,&nbsp;
the north one having a carved gable above.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:58.080
The two towers are unequal in width, the&nbsp;
north tower being wider and having space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.160
for one extra statue in the kings gallery as&nbsp;
well as wider windows in the second level.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.520
These slight irregularities give the composition a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:13.520
vitality and warmth rarely seen in&nbsp;
completely symmetrical buildings.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.200
The front façade of Notre Dame can&nbsp;
be understood as one of the decisive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.080
moments in the evolution of Gothic architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:28.720
because it achieves an unusual degree of&nbsp;
compositional unity and balance in its form.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.800
Notre Dame Cathedral belongs to the&nbsp;
early period of Gothic architecture:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.960
before Notre Dame, only a few&nbsp;
Gothic cathedrals had been built.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.000
And most of them were transitional experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.040
as the Gothic language hadn’t&nbsp;
been fully developed yet.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.400
The abbey at Saint Denis and the&nbsp;
cathedral at Laon had already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.040
developed many of the elements&nbsp;
that would define a Gothic façade.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.920
However, in these earlier examples the&nbsp;
composition still appears fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.000
additive, as though the façade was&nbsp;
assembled through the accumulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.680
of individual innovations that retain&nbsp;
a certain autonomy from the whole.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.280
At Saint Denis, the façade establishes the&nbsp;
standard composition for a Gothic west front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.840
through the three-part organization&nbsp;
and emphasis on vertical hierarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:29.200
but the elements still read as relatively&nbsp;
independent components placed side by side.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.920
At Laon Cathedral, the Gothic vocabulary&nbsp;
becomes far richer and more experimental—porches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.520
topped with gables are introduced above portals,&nbsp;
with triangular turrets between them in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.520
intensify the vertical and pointed character of&nbsp;
the composition, galleries extend horizontally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:56.720
across the façade, and the towers acquire&nbsp;
projecting corners rotated at forty-five degrees.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:03.120
Yet despite its inventiveness, Laon still&nbsp;
conveys this sense of accumulation—architectural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.280
elements seem attached to the building, rather&nbsp;
than designed as part of one coherent system.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.320
The façade seeks completeness through addition.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.440
By contrast, Notre Dame in Paris incorporates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.400
these previously developed elements&nbsp;
into a single coherent composition.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.920
The façade no longer feels assembled, but&nbsp;
instead, all architectural elements appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:36.480
as if carved from a single volume: the portals&nbsp;
are deeply recessed into the mass of the wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:44.320
rather than expressed as projecting porches,&nbsp;
the gable above the left portal seems carved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:51.680
from the plane of the façade rather than appearing&nbsp;
attached to it, and the rose window and flanking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:57.040
openings seem scooped into the stone surface&nbsp;
rather than perforated as in Laon Cathedral.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.880
Even the articulation of elements&nbsp;
remains subordinate to the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.160
compositional order—every element participates in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.920
a regulating geometric organization of&nbsp;
horizontal and vertical relationships.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.840
The result is a remarkable&nbsp;
sense of tectonic unity:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:20.400
the façade behaves not as a collection of Gothic&nbsp;
inventions, but as a single architectural idea.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.880
Notre Dame’s facade represents not the invention,&nbsp;
nor the golden age of Gothic architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:33.120
but the moment in which Gothic façade&nbsp;
achieves its most balanced synthesis.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.320
The experiments and innovations of Saint&nbsp;
Denis and Laon are absorbed, disciplined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:45.600
and unified into a composition in which no element&nbsp;
appears arbitrary or independent from the whole.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.440
In this sense, Notre Dame of Paris&nbsp;
occupies a unique position within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.840
the history of French Gothic architecture.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.320
Later cathedrals would become&nbsp;
increasingly complex, vertical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:04.960
and dematerialized, emphasizing tracery,&nbsp;
perforation, and visual multiplicity.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.760
The wall will gradually dissolve into&nbsp;
structure and ornament, and the façade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:19.920
will become less a carved monumental surface&nbsp;
than a screen of articulated skeletal elements.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.440
This sort of unity and abstraction of&nbsp;
elements into a single architectural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.400
idea that we see clearly expressed in the façade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.000
of Notre Dame is something that’s&nbsp;
also present in the whole building.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.280
A comparison between the plans of the three&nbsp;
churches we’ve discussed so far—Saint Denis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:43.120
Laon, and Notre Dame—immediately reveals&nbsp;
something quite different about Notre Dame:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:49.280
the plan of earlier churches form a Latin cross&nbsp;
with the nave and the projecting transepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.560
but Notre Dame’s transept doesn’t project&nbsp;
beyond the aisle walls—the transept has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.480
subsumed into the body of the building—again,&nbsp;
the building behaves as a single organism.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.400
To achieve this concise plan two key&nbsp;
innovations happen in the interior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:10.240
space of Notre Dame: the addition of two&nbsp;
equal-height-aisles on each side of the nave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.760
and the addition of chapels in the&nbsp;
laterals, between the buttresses.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.680
As you can see, Notre Dame&nbsp;
Cathedral has a very concise form:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.440
the double-span flying buttresses&nbsp;
with the outer chapels placed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:29.520
between integrate the whole program even at a&nbsp;
volumetric level into a single uniform mass.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.920
Because of this spacial configuration, the way&nbsp;
you perceive light and space inside Notre Dame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.000
is very different from earlier churches:&nbsp;
from the moment you enter the building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.840
you immerse yourself into&nbsp;
a dark, gloomy atmosphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.360
almost like a dense forest where light&nbsp;
filters down in fragments from far above.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.720
Light entering through the lower windows&nbsp;
is trapped within the walls of the chapels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:05.600
and hardly reaches the nave after&nbsp;
passing through the double aisles.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.920
To allow more light into the building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.280
the elevation of the interior of the nave was&nbsp;
reorganized from its original composition.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.000
Originally, the elevation consisted of the&nbsp;
columns supporting the arcades on the lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:27.680
level, a tribune above, small oculi on a third&nbsp;
level, and on top, small clerestory windows.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:33.360
Behind these oculi was a space between&nbsp;
the tribune vaults and the wooden roof.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.120
In the thirteenth century, the&nbsp;
tribune roofs were transformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.160
and the oculi were removed in order to&nbsp;
make space for larger clerestory windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:48.400
which were elongated downwards into the&nbsp;
space formerly occupied by the oculi.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.800
Although this third level was eliminated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.920
the four-level composition with the oculi can&nbsp;
still be seen in the bays at the crossing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:00.480
which were returned to their original&nbsp;
design in the nineteenth century.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:08.720
Each bay is articulated by a series of shafts that&nbsp;
rise uninterrupted from the top of each column&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:17.280
to the springing of the vaults, visually&nbsp;
enforcing a strong sense of verticality.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:23.840
The nave is covered by sexpartite vaults,&nbsp;
that is, vaults composed of six parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.320
each vault being capable of covering two bays.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:36.320
The rose windows in Notre Dame’s transept&nbsp;
are particularly special because they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.760
among the largest rose windows from the&nbsp;
Gothic period, and because the transept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:49.760
wings are so short you see the rose windows from&nbsp;
a closer distance than those in other cathedrals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:55.040
thus the awe-inspiring sensation of standing&nbsp;
right beneath Notre Dame’s dazzling rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.040 --> 00:12:06.400
windows within the characteristic darkness of&nbsp;
the interior creates a very powerful contrast.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:12.320
Yet even with the enlargement of the clerestory&nbsp;
and with the addition of huge rose windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:17.440
Notre Dame definitely still falls&nbsp;
within the category of dark churches.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:23.840
This dark atmosphere surrounded by the&nbsp;
scarce light of day that’s capable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:28.800
unevenly reaching the main spaces is&nbsp;
what gives Notre Dame’s interior its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.320
most distinctive feature: that&nbsp;
of solemnity and mysticism.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.680
Today most of the light inside the cathedral&nbsp;
is modern heterogeneous artificial lighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:45.120
so the effect is not as easily&nbsp;
perceived as it was in older times.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.360
The western towers of Notre Dame&nbsp;
Cathedral culminate in flat platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:04.240
unlike the openwork spires of&nbsp;
other cathedrals such as Chartres.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.800
Each face of the towers is pierced&nbsp;
by tall, twin lancet openings that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:17.120
project across the city the acoustic&nbsp;
resonance of the cathedral’s bells.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:20.560
The internal engineering of&nbsp;
the towers is remarkable.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:25.600
At ground level, the bases of the towers&nbsp;
are integrated directly into the interior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:32.000
forming ribbed-vaulted bays that&nbsp;
initiate the double side-aisles.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.200
Higher up, within dark interior chambers,&nbsp;
the massive bronze bells are suspended not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:44.880
directly from the masonry walls, but within an&nbsp;
independent, timber framework known as beffroi.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.480
This timber structure was ingeniously designed&nbsp;
to absorb the immense dynamic vibrations of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:14:00.160
ringing bells, preventing destructive lateral&nbsp;
thrusts from transferring to the stone walls.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:04.400
Access to these upper chambers and&nbsp;
parapets is provided by tight stone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:13.680
spiral staircases skillfully embedded within&nbsp;
the thickness of the corner pier buttresses.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:18.720
The towers are tied together externally by&nbsp;
the delicate openwork screen known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:34.160
Galerie des Chimères, decorated with hand-carved&nbsp;
crochets and perched gargoyles along the parapets.

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:38.240
Behind this pierced screen&nbsp;
is a space that connects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:41.920
two towers with the roof structure of the nave.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:46.800
Here, you find yourself surrounded&nbsp;
by the cathedral's masonry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:53.600
close enough to appreciate the&nbsp;
extraordinary detail of the stonework.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:59.680
The imposing towers create an overwhelming&nbsp;
sense of verticality, yet this is relieved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:08.560
the open arcade of slender columns to the&nbsp;
west, which frames glimpses of the city.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:18.000
Resting directly above the cathedral's&nbsp;
nave is a pitched timber roof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:24.240
designed to protect the stone&nbsp;
vaults from the elements.

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:29.040
This timber structure, is known&nbsp;
as “la forêt”, the forest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:32.720
because is was built from thousands of oak trees.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:36.480
Inside, you can appreciate the&nbsp;
complexity of its carpentry:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:44.400
an elaborate framework of interlocking&nbsp;
timber beams held together by wooden pegs.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.880
The roof was designed in a steep&nbsp;
55-degree pitch and covered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.120
heavy lead sheets that could discharge&nbsp;
the weight of rain and snow to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:59.600
flying buttresses which would expel&nbsp;
the water through the gargoyles.

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:11.120
From the top you get a privileged view&nbsp;
of the roof and spire of Notre Dame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:25.120
but also a privileged view of Paris and the&nbsp;
Île de la Cité, and its principal landmarks.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:29.760
As magnificent as Notre Dame may be,&nbsp;
the cathedral was nearly demolished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:34.400
in the 19th century, and it was&nbsp;
saved by no less than a book.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:40.640
In 1831, Victor Hugo published his&nbsp;
novel Notre Dame de Paris, where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:45.120
not only narrates through the story&nbsp;
of Quasimodo, the hunchback, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:54.240
uses the medieval setting of the cathedral of&nbsp;
Notre Dame as another character in the story.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:58.960
The glorious time of Gothic architecture had&nbsp;
ended after the Middle Ages, and with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:04.400
rise of the Renaissance across Europe, people&nbsp;
began to consider medieval buildings barbaric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:11.840
and deformed monstrosities: calling a Renaissance&nbsp;
building “Gothic” was an insult to the architect.

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:17.840
In the time of Victor Hugo, Notre Dame Cathedral&nbsp;
was a very degraded and mutilated building, both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:24.240
by the action of time and the hand of man: Time&nbsp;
had left many marks and dents to the architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:30.720
but man had destroyed it: in the interior by&nbsp;
replacing the colorful and beautiful medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:39.840
stained glass windows with white panes of&nbsp;
glass, replacing the medieval Gothic altar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:48.240
splendidly charged with shrines and reliquaries,&nbsp;
with a ponderous baroque marble sarcophagus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.440
hiding the stone pointed arches in the&nbsp;
choir with the marble rounded arches seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.440 --> 00:18:02.400
in Napoleon’s coronation, and those of the nave&nbsp;
with paintings; refacing the original paving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:11.520
carved with tombstones of bishops and knights,&nbsp;
with the chess tiles we see today, and destroying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:16.640
the colossal statue of Saint Christopher that&nbsp;
stood in the entrance of the cathedral, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:22.960
countless statues which peopled all the spaces&nbsp;
between the columns of the nave and the choir.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:26.960
And on the exterior by scooping out a&nbsp;
new pointed arch in the middle of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:33.760
central portal and replacing the original&nbsp;
doors with a Rococo door with arabesques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:37.520
tearing down the spire in the middle of&nbsp;
the crossing of the nave and transept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:42.960
which soared into the sky, farther even than the&nbsp;
towers, not to mention the French Revolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.040
cause of the destruction of the statues&nbsp;
on the façade, particularly those on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:56.880
royal gallery depicting the kings, and those&nbsp;
on the portals, leaving the niches empty.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:01.360
Thus had the marvelous art of the Middle&nbsp;
Ages been treated in every country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:05.440
especially France, even to the point&nbsp;
that demolishing Notre Dame Cathedral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:10.480
was seriously considered in the&nbsp;
19th century to build a new church.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:16.960
In his book, Victor Hugo gives an exalting speech&nbsp;
of how glorious had the medieval past been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:20.320
describing the environment of how&nbsp;
the cathedral and the city would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:27.200
appeared back in the Middle Ages, glorious&nbsp;
buildings and castles even more splendorous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:34.160
legendary, and enchanting than the Alhambra in&nbsp;
Spain, with towers and steeples and churches.

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:39.120
Victor Hugo’s descriptions are certainly&nbsp;
characterized by a Romantic exaggeration.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:42.800
I mean I’m sure the spindly towers at&nbsp;
the Palace of the Tournelles that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:46.640
describes must have been glorious,&nbsp;
but he never saw them in person as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:50.480
they had been demolished, and&nbsp;
he never visited the Alhambra.

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:55.680
Nevertheless, this glorious speech praising&nbsp;
the architecture of the medieval Paris was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:00.640
setting for the story of the hunchbacked&nbsp;
bell-ringer, and so famous and popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:06.800
did the novel become across Europe, that&nbsp;
intellectuals, painters and writers began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:18.000
to include Notre Dame into their itinerary, and&nbsp;
Notre Dame Cathedral became a symbol of Paris.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.800
Such a great prestige did&nbsp;
the building acquire thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:25.760
to Victor Hugo that interest impulsed&nbsp;
a full restoration of the building.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:32.080
A national competition was launched in&nbsp;
1844 to rescue the decaying monument.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:38.000
Architect Eugene Emmanuel Viollet-le-Duc won the&nbsp;
competition and approached the cathedral through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:43.280
his famous doctrine of restoration which was to&nbsp;
restore a building to what he believed was its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:48.560
ideal Gothic state—sometimes completing&nbsp;
things that had never actually existed.

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:55.200
His intervention sought to restore Notre Dame&nbsp;
to an idealized, stylistically unified building.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:59.520
Externally, Viollet-le-Duc completely&nbsp;
rebuilt and consolidated the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:04.480
of flying buttresses to stabilize the high vaults.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:10.400
He also rebuilt the iconic timber-and-lead&nbsp;
central spire over the crossing—replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:16.240
the original medieval spire that had been&nbsp;
dismantled in the late eighteenth century—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:22.720
added the famous array of gargoyles&nbsp;
and chimeras along the upper parapets.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:29.280
Internally, his most remarkable&nbsp;
decision was the reconstruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:33.920
of the original four-story elevation&nbsp;
in the bays adjacent to the crossing.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:39.840
By re-inserting the round oculi level&nbsp;
beneath the clerestory in these specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:45.680
bays, Viollet-le-Duc created a living&nbsp;
architectural diagram within the church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:49.440
allowing the viewer to visually&nbsp;
compare the cathedral’s original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:58.880
twelfth-century four-tier disposition with&nbsp;
its thirteenth-century enlarged elevation.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:03.520
To build the new spire, Viollet le Duc,&nbsp;
applied his restoration philosophy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:08.880
instead of trying to replicate the original&nbsp;
design, of which remained only a few imprecise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:14.720
drawings and engravings, he created a&nbsp;
new design inspired by the best examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:24.640
of original medieval spires such as that in&nbsp;
Amiens Cathedral and in the Sainte Chapelle.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:37.040
It was a 96-meter-high timber framework&nbsp;
encased in over 250 tons of lead sheeting.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:42.720
At its octagonal base, he arrayed sixteen&nbsp;
copper statues depicting the Twelve Apostles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:50.560
and the Four Evangelists descending along the&nbsp;
roof slopes all looking outwards to the city.

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:55.040
Among them, the figure of Saint&nbsp;
Thomas—patron saint of architects—was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.040 --> 00:23:00.480
sculpted with Viollet-le-Duc's&nbsp;
face, gazing upward at his creation.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:04.880
Atop the spire, a copper rooster&nbsp;
filled with sacred relics served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:08.000
as a symbolic lightning rod to protect the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:14.960
permanently restoring the cathedral’s&nbsp;
vertical axis against the Parisian skyline.

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:18.960
The Cathedral we see in Paris&nbsp;
today is Viollet-le-Duc’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:22.360
interpretation of Medieval Notre Dame.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:28.400
Even the roof and spire, rebuilt after the fire in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:36.640
2019, are reproductions that follow&nbsp;
Viollet le Duc’s original drawings.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:42.080
If you want to see how this remarkably coherent&nbsp;
system of Gothic architecture was then refined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:46.640
becoming increasingly sophisticated,&nbsp;
dematerialized, and monumental with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:50.080
even taller structures and&nbsp;
larger expanses of glass.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:52.480
I recommend you watching my video on Chartres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:57.600
Cathedral where I explain how Gothic&nbsp;
architecture evolved even further.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.680
Thanks for watching, I hope&nbsp;
you enjoyed and learned.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:04.400
Please like this video, subscribe to my channel,&nbsp;
and I'll see you very soon in another episode.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:05.200
Goodbye.

